// ═══ JAVA 7: HOW THE INFINITE LOOP HAPPENS ═══
// HashMap.resize() in Java 7: transfer() method
// Rehashes entries from old table to new table using HEAD INSERTION:
//
// Old bucket: A → B → null
// After transfer (single-thread): B → A → null (REVERSED!)
//
// Two threads transferring simultaneously:
// Thread-1: reads A.next = B
// Thread-2: transfers B first → new bucket: B → null
// Thread-2: transfers A → new bucket: A → B → null
// Thread-1: resumes → sets A.next = B (already done by Thread-2)
// Thread-1: transfers B → sets B.next = A
// Result: A → B → A → B → ... CIRCULAR!
//
// Any get() that lands in this bucket: traverses FOREVER!
// 100% CPU on this thread. No exception. No crash. Just STUCK.

// ═══ JAVA 8+: HEAD INSERTION REMOVED, BUT STILL UNSAFE ═══
// Java 8 uses TAIL insertion during resize → no reversal → no circular list.
// BUT concurrent puts can still:
// 1. Two threads both see bucket as empty → both insert → one LOST!
// 2. Tree bin corruption: concurrent treeify → broken red-black tree
// 3. Size counter: non-atomic increment → wrong size()
// 4. Null pointer: corrupted node reference → NPE in get()
// No infinite loop, but DATA CORRUPTION — wrong results, lost entries.

// ═══ WHY NO EXCEPTION? ═══
// The HashMap code doesn't CHECK for concurrent modification during get/put!
// (modCount is only checked by iterators — fail-fast)
// get() just follows node.next pointers → if circular → infinite loop
// No bounds check, no timeout, no detection mechanism.
// The thread is ALIVE (running CPU instructions) but makes NO PROGRESS.

// ═══ THREAD DUMP: how to detect ═══
// jstack <PID> shows the stuck thread:
// "http-nio-8080-exec-5" RUNNABLE
//   at java.util.HashMap$TreeNode.find(HashMap.java:1880)  ← stuck here!
//   at java.util.HashMap.get(HashMap.java:556)
// Thread state: RUNNABLE (not BLOCKED/WAITING!) — it's running but looping!
// This is why it's so hard to detect — it looks "healthy" in monitoring.

// ═══ ConcurrentHashMap: no corruption possible ═══
ConcurrentHashMap<String, String> safe = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();
// Resize: coordinated via transferIndex (multiple threads help, but synchronized!)
// Put: CAS for empty bucket, synchronized(firstNode) for non-empty
// Get: volatile read — no lock, always consistent snapshot
// NO circular lists. NO data corruption. NO infinite loops.
